syntax: keep '#' after a word part inside command substitutions

A '#' starts a comment only at the beginning of a word, so in

	x="$(nix eval "$dir"#attr)"

the #attr belongs to the argument. The lexer knew this at the top level,
where words are parsed with the unquotedWordCont quote state, but inside
$(...), backquotes and ${ ...;} the quote state stays subCmd, so the '#'
after a closing quote began a comment that swallowed the rest of the
line, and the substitution and the outer quotes were never closed.

The parser now tracks that it is in the middle of a word independently
of the quote state, resetting it for nested commands so that a '#' at
the start of a word inside $( still starts a comment.

Reproducer, before this change: `x=$(echo "a"#b)` failed with
"reached EOF without matching `$(` with `)`".

